# Standard base64 as `torrent-add`'s `metainfo` carries it. The target asserts
# a canonical round trip, so the tokens that matter are the ones where two
# spellings of one value could both be accepted: padding, the alphabet's edges,
# and the whitespace a MIME-wrapping encoder inserts.
"="
"=="
"A"
"AA"
"AAA"
"AAAA"
"+"
"/"
"+/+/"
"////"
"++++"
# The two characters the I2P alphabet uses in place of `+` and `/`. A decoder
# that shared a table with `i2pnet::addr` would accept these.
"-"
"~"
"-~-~"
# MIME wrapping, which is the one leniency the decoder has.
"\n"
"\r\n"
" "
"\t"
"AAAA\nAAAA"
# Bencode openers, so a corpus entry can grow toward a real .torrent — which is
# what the decoded bytes are handed to next.
"ZDg6"
"ZDQ6aW5mb2Q="
"d8:announce"
"d4:infod"
"ZjE6"
# Length edges around the four-symbol quantum.
"Zg=="
"Zm8="
"Zm9v"
"Zm9vYg=="
"Zm9vYmE="
"Zm9vYmFy"
